;; Guest browser desktop input method: one key press -> the hosted IME's
;; decision -> text committed into the focused window's body, on KERNEL.ELF
;; (ADR-0225).
;;
;; WHY THIS IS A KOTOBA OBJECT AND NOT A C TABLE
;;
;; C reads Linux key codes off the virtio-keyboard ring (mechanism). Which
;; character a key is, how romaji becomes kana, when a reading becomes a
;; kanji and where committed text goes are input-method and browser
;; decisions (browser.input `:text/input` goes to the focused window).
;;
;; THE ORACLE is src/aiueos/compositor/ime.cljk, and this object mirrors its
;; `handle-key` branch by branch, quirks included:
;;   letter     appended to the romaji buffer, then `convert-buf`: greedy
;;              longest mora (3, 2, 1 bytes) from the start, sokuon (a doubled
;;              k s t p c g z d b j) emits small tsu; what does not convert
;;              stays in the buffer
;;   Backspace  cancels a conversion, else drops the last romaji byte, else the
;;              last preedit code point, else -- nothing is being composed --
;;              deletes the code point before the caret in the focused body
;;              (browser.text-edit delete-backward; ADR-0232). Here the
;;              object leaves its oracle on purpose: the hosted `handle-key`
;;              consumes that Backspace and does nothing, but browser.input
;;              maps Backspace to :text/edit :delete-backward, and a key the
;;              IME has no use for belongs to the page.
;;   Escape     cancels a conversion (preedit back to its reading), else
;;              clears buffer and preedit
;;   Space      flushes (a lone "n" becomes "nn"), then converts the whole
;;              preedit -- a reading of any length -- that the dictionary
;;              knows to its first candidate, or cycles the candidates;
;;              otherwise commits the preedit (ADR-0235)
;;   Enter      flushes and commits the preedit
;;   other keys ignored ("-", "," and "." are in the oracle's table but its
;;              letter test never lets them in, so they are ignored here too)
;;   IME off    a letter goes straight to the window as latin, Backspace
;;              deletes as above (ADR-0232); the rest is ignored
;;   Toggle     Hankaku/Zenkaku (evdev 41), in either state: the IME turns off
;;              if on, on if off, and whatever was being composed -- romaji,
;;              preedit, a conversion -- is discarded, not committed. The
;;              hosted `handle-key` has no toggle key; this is
;;              aiueos.compositor.desktop `set-ime`, which the hosted desktop
;;              calls with the flipped state (ADR-0228).
;;   Alt+Tab    the window manager's, not the page's (ADR-0237). Alt (evdev 56)
;;              is held from its press to its release in word 431; a Tab
;;              (evdev 15) press while it is held answers 256 + the id of the
;;              window at the BOTTOM of the stack (word 5) and touches nothing
;;              else -- no body, no composition, no IME word. C hands that id
;;              to browser-reduce.kotoba as a :window/focus (kind 5), which
;;              raises it: raising the bottom rotates the stack, so n presses
;;              visit every window once and come back to the first. The hosted
;;              desktop has the host's window manager and browser.input no
;;              shortcut, so the rule is the kernel's (no oracle). Alt itself
;;              and a Tab without Alt reach the IME as before (it ignores both);
;;              every other key with Alt held is typed as without it.
;;   Selection  (ADR-0240) Shift (evdev 42 or 54) is held in word 2035 from its
;;              press to its release, as Alt is in 431. Shift+Left (105) and
;;              Shift+Right (106) are browser.text-edit move-caret -1 / +1
;;              with :extend? on the focused body, whose caret is at its end
;;              until a selection moves it: the anchor stays at the end, the
;;              caret moves, so the selection is always the last k code
;;              points [n - k, n) and the caret sits at n - k. k is word 2037,
;;              the window it belongs to word 2036 (0 and 0: none); a
;;              selection of another window reads as none and is replaced by
;;              the first Shift+Left here (`:one-selection-at-a-time`). k is
;;              clamped to the body (normalize-selection); at 0 or at n the
;;              key changes nothing. While something is composed the arrows
;;              are the composition's and do nothing (`:no-arrows-in-a-
;;              composition`). Backspace with a selection is delete-backward's
;;              first branch: insert-text "" over it -- the k code points go
;;              and the caret is at the end again. A letter with a selection
;;              first does the same (insert-text replaces the selection; with
;;              the IME on, the composition starts over the emptied place, as a
;;              platform IME's compositionstart deletes the selection), then
;;              goes on as before. Plain Left / Right, Home, End are still not
;;              taken (`:caret-only-at-the-end` holds outside a selection), and
;;              Shift does not case a letter (`:shift-does-not-case-letters`).
;;   Clipboard  (ADR-0241) Ctrl (evdev 29 or 97) is held in word 2038 from its
;;              press to its release, as Shift is in 2035. With Ctrl held a
;;              C (46) press over a selection, nothing composed, is a
;;              :clipboard/write request and a V (47) press, nothing composed,
;;              a :clipboard/read request -- browser.desktop-backend `request`:
;;              the key answers 512 (write) or 513 (read) and changes nothing,
;;              because whether the host's clipboard may be touched is the
;;              permission broker's (broker-admit.kotoba, ADR-0096), not the
;;              page's. Only when the broker admits :clipboard does C hand the
;;              object the completion: the same code with value 3 (a device
;;              never sends 3; the keyboard ring passes 0..2 only) and, in
;;              place of the dictionary, the clipboard -- 260 bytes, word 0 its
;;              length L (0..64), words 1..L its code points. A write
;;              completion copies the selected code points into it (the
;;              selection's text, as browser.text-edit keeps it); a read
;;              completion is insert-text of its text: the selection goes and
;;              the text is appended at the caret, which is the end of the
;;              body -- whole or not at all (-5). A refused request has no
;;              completion, so nothing is copied or pasted (fail-closed, as
;;              desktop-backend `request` produces no effect on a deny).
;;              Every other press with Ctrl held changes nothing
;;              (`:no-other-ctrl-shortcuts`: Ctrl+A select-all is not taken);
;;              C with nothing selected, or C / V while something is composed,
;;              asks for nothing (`:no-clipboard-in-a-composition`).
;; THE DICTIONARY is os/aiueos/ime/aiueos-kanji.dic (aiueos-dict/v1, written
;; by os/aiueos/scripts/gen-ime-dictionary.cljk from Unicode Unihan; the
;; format is described in that script's header), handed in by C from the
;; initramfs as it hands the font. The oracle reads the same file. A reading
;; is found by binary search over the sorted records; while it is being
;; converted, word 444 holds its record's word offset, so Escape and
;; Backspace put the reading back from the dictionary, as the oracle puts
;; back `:reading`. With no dictionary (a null region or 0 bytes) nothing
;; converts and Space commits kana, as the oracle does with `:kanji? false`.
;; The romaji table between the GENERATED markers is written from the
;; oracle's own `mora` by os/aiueos/scripts/gen-ime-mora.cljk (--check).
;;
;; Temporary native constraints (ADR-0223's removal condition): word types,
;; at most five parameters, and bounded buffers the oracle does not have --
;; 8 romaji bytes, 32 preedit code points, the window body's 64. A letter
;; into a full romaji buffer and a commit into a body without room are
;; refused with the state unchanged; kana past the 32nd preedit code point
;; are dropped and the key answers -8.
;;
;; STATE, in the browser-frame2 surface (u32 words, 8192 bytes):
;;   0..31 browser state (focus at 2)   160 + 64(id-1) body of window id
;;   432 IME on   433 romaji length   434..441 romaji bytes
;;   442 converting   443 candidate index   444 word offset of the reading's
;;   dictionary record   445 preedit length   446..477 preedit code points
;;   431 Alt held (ADR-0237; 1 held, else not -- the word frame2's scratch
;;   left unused, 416..430)
;;   478 overflow flag   479 the dictionary's bytes (written on every call,
;;   so a read of the dictionary is bounded by it, as frame2 keeps the
;;   font's at 423)
;;   2035 Shift held (ADR-0240; 1 held, else not)   2036 the window id the
;;   selection belongs to (0 none)   2037 its length, the code points before
;;   the end of that window's body that are selected -- three of the words
;;   freed by cutting frame2's op capacity from 260 to 259
;;   2038 Ctrl held (ADR-0241; 1 held, else not). The clipboard is not in
;;   the surface: it is the host's, handed in only with an admitted
;;   completion. 2039 was the surface's last unassigned word; ADR-0247 took it
;;   (the high-contrast palette, F10).
;;
;; ABI: [surface surface-bytes dictionary dictionary-bytes key] where key is
;; the evdev key code * 4 + its value (0 release, 1 press, 2 repeat) -- five
;; parameters is the native ceiling, so code and value share one -- ->
;; code points committed to the focused window by this key (0 for most
;; keys; at most 64, a body's size), or 256 + a window id for Alt+Tab (a
;; :window/focus for browser-reduce, ADR-0237), or 512 / 513 for Ctrl+C /
;; Ctrl+V (a :clipboard/write / :clipboard/read request for the broker,
;; ADR-0241); for a completion (value 3, the clipboard in place of the
;; dictionary) the code points copied into the clipboard or pasted into the
;; body; or
;;   -1 null region / surface not 8192 bytes   -2 abi or n outside 1..4
;;   -3 focus is not a window id 1..4          -5 the body would pass 64
;;   -6 a dictionary is given and is not aiueos-dict/v1 (header), or the
;;      record a conversion needs is out of its bounds
;;   -7 a completion's clipboard is a null region, not 260 bytes, or its
;;      length word is past 64
;;   -8 the romaji buffer is full, or the preedit overflowed
;; A release or repeat (value != 1) changes nothing and returns 0, except
;; Alt's release, which lets go of word 431, Shift's, of word 2035, and
;; Ctrl's, of word 2038. A header
;; refusal changes nothing; a record refusal on Space comes after the flush,
;; so the romaji is in the preedit and nothing is committed or converted.

(ns aiueos.browser-ime
  (:export [aiueos-browser-key main]))

(defn w [s i]
  (kernel-load-u32 (kernel-subregion s 8192 (* i 4) 4) 4 0))

(defn wp [s i v]
  (kernel-store-u32 (kernel-subregion s 8192 (* i 4) 4) 4 0 v))

